      "text": "Given the functions \\( f(x) = ax + b \\) and \\( g(x) = 2x - 5 \\), we know that \\( g(f(x)) = 3x + 4 \\). We need to find the constants \\( a \\) and \\( b \\).\n\nFirst, substitute \\( f(x) = ax + b \\) into \\( g(x) \\):\n\n\\[ g(f(x)) = g(ax + b) \\]\n\nSince \\( g(x) = 2x - 5 \\), we have:\n\n\\[ g(ax + b) = 2(ax + b) - 5 \\]\n\nSimplify the right-hand side:\n\n\\[ g(ax + b) = 2ax + 2b - 5 \\]\n\nWe are given that \\( g(f(x)) = 3x + 4 \\). Therefore, we can equate the two expressions:\n\n\\[ 2ax + 2b - 5 = 3x + 4 \\]\n\nFor these two expressions to be equal for all \\( x \\), the coefficients of \\( x \\) and the constant terms must be equal. This gives us the following system of equations:\n\n1. \\( 2a = 3 \\)\n2. \\( 2b - 5 = 4 \\)\n\nSolve the first equation for \\( a \\):\n\n\\[ 2a = 3 \\]\n\\[ a = \\frac{3}{2} \\]\n\nSolve the second equation for \\( b \\):\n\n\\[ 2b - 5 = 4 \\]\n\\[ 2b = 9 \\]\n\\[ b = \\frac{9}{2} \\]\n\nNow, we have \\( a = \\frac{3}{2} \\) and \\( b = \\frac{9}{2} \\). To find \\( a + b \\):\n\n\\[ a + b = \\frac{3}{2} + \\frac{9}{2} = \\frac{12}{2} = 6 \\]\n\nThus, the value of \\( a + b \\) is \\(\\boxed{6}\\).",
