      "text": "To solve for \\(a\\) and \\(b\\), we start by substituting the expression for \\(f(x)\\) into \\(g(x)\\). Given \\(f(x) = ax + b\\) and \\(g(x) = 2x - 5\\), we have:\n\n\\[ g(f(x)) = g(ax + b) = 2(ax + b) - 5. \\]\n\nNext, we expand and simplify the right-hand side of the equation:\n\n\\[ g(f(x)) = 2(ax + b) - 5 = 2ax + 2b - 5. \\]\n\nWe are given that \\(g(f(x)) = 3x + 4\\). Therefore, we can set the two expressions equal to each other:\n\n\\[ 2ax + 2b - 5 = 3x + 4. \\]\n\nFor the equation to hold for all \\(x\\), the coefficients of \\(x\\) and the constant terms on both sides must be equal. This gives us the following system of equations:\n\n1. \\(2a = 3\\) (coefficient of \\(x\\)),\n2. \\(2b - 5 = 4\\) (constant term).\n\nFirst, solve for \\(a\\):\n\n\\[ 2a = 3 \\implies a = \\frac{3}{2}. \\]\n\nNext, solve for \\(b\\):\n\n\\[ 2b - 5 = 4 \\implies 2b = 9 \\implies b = \\frac{9}{2}. \\]\n\nNow, we need to find the value of \\(a + b\\):\n\n\\[ a + b = \\frac{3}{2} + \\frac{9}{2} = \\frac{3 + 9}{2} = \\frac{12}{2} = 6. \\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\(a + b\\) is \\(\\boxed{6}\\).",
